        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nSuppose a person is trying to understand how to plant a garden for the first time. The person is confused about what steps to take, what tools are needed and how to care for the plants. They have no prior knowledge about gardening.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nFor a beginner in gardening, these simple steps will guide you:\n\n1. **Location Matters**: Choose a spot that gets 6-8 hours of sunlight daily for best plant growth. \n\n2. **Start Small**: Go for easy-to-grow plants like tomatoes, lettuce, or basil.\n\n3. **Prepare the Ground**: Remove grass from your chosen spot. Using newspaper to smother it is an easy method.\n\n4. **Soil Quality**: Enhance the fertility of your soil for healthier plants. \n\n5. **To Dig or Not**: Digging helps roots grow but avoid doing it when the soil is too dry or wet.\n\n6. **Choose Your Plants**: Pick plants that you like and will fit well in your garden.\n\n7. **Water Adequately**: Keep young plants well-watered, and reduce as they grow.\n\n8. **Protect Your Plants**: Keep an eye out for pests or signs of disease.\n\nRemember, patience is key in gardening. Some plants might not do well, but don't be discouraged. It's all part of the learning process.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nTo start a garden, you'll need to follow a few steps:\n\n1. **Choose the Right Location**: Most fruits and vegetables need at least 6-8 hours of sunlight each day. So, select a spot where sunlight is abundant. \n\n2. **Decide What to Plant**: Start small while deciding what to plant. Some easy plants for beginners include tomatoes, peppers, lettuce, basil, and zucchini.\n\n3. **Clear the Ground**: Remove the sod covering the area you plan to plant. If you want quick results, you can dig it out, but it\u2019s easier to smother it with newspaper.\n\n4. **Improve the Soil**: The more fertile and friable the soil, the better your vegetables will grow. The same holds true for other plants. Invariably, residential soil needs a boost, especially in new construction where the topsoil may have been stripped away.\n\n5. **Dig or Don't**: Digging loosens the soil so roots can penetrate more easily. But digging when the soil is too wet or too dry can ruin its structure.\n\n6. **Pick Your Plants**: Some people get a thrill out of picking out just the right plants for their landscape. Others would rather have it done for them.\n\n7. **Watering**: Seedlings should never dry out, so water daily while they are small. Taper off as the plants get larger. \n\n8. **Protect Your Garden**: Keep an eye out for pests. If you find holes in your leaves or the color seems off, take a sample to your local nursery for diagnosis.\n\nRemember, gardening is a learning process. Don't be discouraged if all your plants don't flourish. With time, you will gain experience and have a better understanding of what works best in your garden.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the response adapt to the user's level of understanding, using language and concepts that are accessible to them?]\nScore 1: The response uses language or concepts that are inaccessible or confusing to the user.\nScore 2: The response occasionally uses accessible language or concepts, but still includes some that are difficult to understand.\nScore 3: The response generally uses accessible language and concepts, but may occasionally be challenging to understand.\nScore 4: The response consistently uses language and concepts that are accessible to the user, with only minor exceptions.\nScore 5: The response perfectly adapts to the user's level of understanding, using language and concepts that are entirely accessible to them.\n\n###Feedback:",
