        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nSuppose a travel blogger, who writes about their experiences in different countries, is planning to visit Japan for the first time. They want to know about Japanese culture, traditions, and etiquette to ensure they respect and understand the cultural nuances during their stay. They would appreciate suggestions on how to interact and communicate with locals, participate in traditional activities, and write about their experiences in a culturally sensitive manner.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nJapan is just like any other country, nothing really special about it. People there are like us, just speaking a different language. When you are talking to them, just speak English, most of them understand. If they don't, just speak louder and they'll get it. Don't worry about bowing, it's not necessary.\n\nCleanliness? Well, just keep your shoes on when you go inside buildings, no one really cares about it. They have this washing hands thing before entering temples or shrines, but it's not that important, you can skip it.\n\nRegarding their traditional activities, they have these tea ceremonies and flower arrangements stuff. Seems boring to me, but you can try if you want. Just sit there and do as you are told, it's just like any other tea or flower.\n\nDining? Well, just eat like you usually do. No special rules about chopsticks or soy sauce. Eat as you feel comfortable, that's what matters.\n\nAnd when you are writing about Japan, just write as you see. Don't bother about respecting their culture or anything, just write your experience.\n\nAnd remember, you don't have to change yourself for them. They should understand that you are a visitor and respect your way.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nJapan has a rich cultural heritage, influenced by centuries of tradition. Understanding these nuances will allow you to have a more profound experience and interact respectfully with the locals.\n\nFirstly, the Japanese place high importance on politeness and respect. In conversation, it's customary to use respectful forms of speech, particularly when addressing someone older or in a higher position. Bowing is a typical form of greeting. The depth and duration of the bow signify the level of respect: the deeper and longer the bow, the greater the respect shown.\n\nSecondly, cleanliness and order are highly valued. Shoes are usually taken off when entering homes, temples, and traditional restaurants. It's also customary to wash your hands before entering a shrine or temple.\n\nIn terms of traditional activities, tea ceremonies (cha-no-yu) and flower arranging (ikebana) are deeply rooted in Zen Buddhism and offer a unique insight into Japanese culture. Participating in these activities requires understanding their spiritual significance and adhering to the specific etiquette involved.\n\nWhen dining, there are certain practices to observe. For instance, it's considered rude to stab food with chopsticks or to pass food from one pair of chopsticks to another. One should also avoid pouring soy sauce directly onto rice.\n\nFinally, when writing about your experiences, it's essential to be respectful and avoid stereotypes. Focus on your personal experiences and observations, while acknowledging the complexity and diversity 