        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nSuppose that a software developer is seeking to understand the best practices and possible approaches to implement a secure authentication system in a web application. What are the critical considerations, potential methods, and industry-standard terminologies that should be used in explaining these aspects?\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nWhen you're trying to make a login thing for your website, there's a lot you gotta think about. First, you gotta make it hard for bad guys to break in. Like, think about it as if you're building a castle. You want lots of walls and stuff so if one part gets busted, the rest is still okay.\n\nThen, when people are talking to your website, make sure nobody can listen in. It's like passing a secret note in class. You want to make sure it gets to your friend without anyone else seeing it.\n\nAlso, don't keep passwords like, written down or something. Make them look different, like, scramble them up so if someone finds them, they won't know what they are. And, like, use different spices for each one to make it even harder to guess.\n\nAnd don't give people more access than they need. It's like, if someone is house-sitting for you, you wouldn't give them the keys to your car and your bank vault too, right?\n\nAlso, it's good to have, like, a secret handshake or something. Not just a password, but also something else that only the person would know or have.\n\nAnd make sure your website can't be tricked into doing stuff it's not supposed to. Like, make it so it won't just do whatever anyone tells it to.\n\nAnd remember, there's no such thing as perfect security, but doing these things will help a lot.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nWhen designing a secure authentication system for a web application, several critical factors must be taken into consideration. \n\nFirstly, the concept of \"Defense in Depth\" should be fundamental to your approach. This term, originating from military strategy, suggests that security should be multi-layered, so if one layer is breached, others are still in place. \n\nOne layer of this defense strategy is \"Secure Transmission\", which means that the data transmitted between the client and the server should be encrypted using protocols like HTTPS (Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure) to prevent eavesdropping attacks.\n\nAnother layer is \"Secure Password Storage\". Never store passwords in plaintext. Instead, use a strong, one-way cryptographic hash function like bcrypt or Argon2 to hash the passwords before storing them. Also, consider using a unique, random \"Salt\" for each user's password to prevent rainbow table attacks.\n\nNext, consider the concept of \"Least Privilege\". Each user of the system should have the minimum levels of access \u2013 or privileges \u2013 necessary to complete their tasks. This reduces the potential damage from accidents or successful attacks.\n\nIn addition, \"Two-Factor Authentication\" (2FA) is an additional security layer that requires not only a password and username but also something that only the user has on them, such as a piece of information only they should know or have immediately at hand, like a physical token.\n\nLastly, ensure that your application is immune to \"Cross-Site Scripting\" (XSS) and 