nd \"Cross-Site Request Forgery\" (CSRF) attacks. By implementing these best practices, the security of your authentication system can be significantly improved.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nWhen designing a secure authentication system for a web application, several critical factors must be taken into consideration. \n\nFirstly, the concept of \"Defense in Depth\" should be fundamental to your approach. This term, originating from military strategy, suggests that security should be multi-layered, so if one layer is breached, others are still in place. \n\nOne layer of this defense strategy is \"Secure Transmission\", which means that the data transmitted between the client and the server should be encrypted using protocols like HTTPS (Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure) to prevent eavesdropping attacks.\n\nAnother layer is \"Secure Password Storage\". Never store passwords in plaintext. Instead, use a strong, one-way cryptographic hash function like bcrypt or Argon2 to hash the passwords before storing them. Also, consider using a unique, random \"Salt\" for each user's password to prevent rainbow table attacks.\n\nNext, consider the concept of \"Least Privilege\". Each user of the system should have the minimum levels of access \u2013 or privileges \u2013 necessary to complete their tasks. This reduces the potential damage from accidents or successful attacks.\n\nIn addition, \"Two-Factor Authentication\" (2FA) is an additional security layer that requires not only a password and username but also something that only the user has on them, such as a piece of information only they should know or have immediately at hand, like a physical token.\n\nLastly, ensure that your application is immune to \"Cross-Site Scripting\" (XSS) and \"Cross-Site Request Forgery\" (CSRF) attacks, as these can be used to bypass even the most secure authentication systems. XSS vulnerabilities can be mitigated by properly validating, sanitizing and escaping user inputs, while CSRF at