        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nThere is a situation where a person is seeking advice from a friend who is a renowned psychologist. This person is feeling down because of a recent breakup and is also stressed about an upcoming job interview. The person expresses their feelings in a mix of subtle and apparent emotional cues. How should the friend respond to both offer comfort for the emotional pain and provide useful advice for the job interview?\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nI understand you're in a tough spot right now. The pain from a breakup is real and it's important to acknowledge it, not hide it. Remember, it's okay to take some time for yourself to heal. As for the job interview, it's normal to feel anxious. See this as a chance for growth, not just a stress source. Do your preparation, believe in your skills, and you'll do great. Life has its highs and lows, but these challenging times shape us into better individuals. Hang in there, brighter days are ahead.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nIt sounds like you're going through a challenging time, and it's completely normal to feel this way. Breakups can be really tough, and it's important to allow yourself to feel the pain, rather than suppressing it. You're not alone in this and it's okay to take some time for yourself. Remember, it's okay to grieve, and it's okay to cry. Everyone goes through tough times, and it's these times that make us stronger.\n\nAs for your job interview, it's natural to feel nervous. It's a big opportunity and you want to do your best. Try to see this as an opportunity to learn and grow, instead of a source of stress. Do your research about the company, practice common interview questions, and, most importantly, believe in yourself. You've got this! \n\nRemember, it's okay to feel down, but do not let these feelings dominate your present and future. Life is full of ups and downs, and it's the challenging times that contribute to our growth. You're a strong person, and this too shall pass. Keep faith in yourself and remember, every cloud has a silver lining.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the response adapt its language and tone based on the user's input? Consideration should be given to whether the response is able to pick up on the user's emotions and adjust its language accordingly.]\nScore 1: The response does not recognise the user's emotions at all and uses a uniform language and tone across all responses.\nScore 2: The response slightly adapts its language and tone based on the user's emotions but in a very limited and inconsistent way.\nScore 3: The response does a fair job at adapting its language and tone based on the user's emotions but could still improve in terms of consistency and range of emotions recognised.\nScore 4: The response consistently adapts its language and tone based on the user's emotions but may still miss out on a few subtle cues.\nScore 5: The response perfectly recognises the user's emotions and adjusts its language and tone accordingly in a seamless and natural manner.\n\n###Feedback:",
