lement is like. \n\nIn the world of tiny particles, two particles can become entangled. This means that, no matter how far apart they are, a change to one instantly affects the other. It's as if they are two halves of a whole, always in sync. This isn't because they are sending messages to each other faster than light, but because they are deeply connected in a way that's hard to explain using our everyday experiences.\n\nNow, you're probably wondering, \"How does this apply to real life?\" That's a great question! Scientists are still figuring out all the ways we can use this, but one area that's looking promising is in communication. If we can use entangled particles to send information, it could be done almost instantaneously and could be super secure. Imagine being able to send a message to someone on the other side of the world with no delay! That's just one possible application and researchers are busy exploring many more.\n\nI hope that makes it a bit clearer! Remember, it's okay if you don't get it all at once. Quantum physics is famously said to be so strange that even scientists don't totally understand it. So, you're in good company!\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the response demonstrate the ability to adapt language and tone based on the user's input (e.g., formal vs. informal, technical vs. non-technical)?]\nScore 1: The response does not adapt language or tone to the user's input, resulting in communication that may feel out of place or inappropriate.\nScore 2: The response shows limited ability to adapt language or tone, occasionally matching the user's input but often missing the mark.\nScore 3: The response demonstrates some ability to adapt language and tone, aligning with the user's input more often than not.\nScore 4: The response often adapts language and tone to the user's input, creating a natural and appropriate dialogue.\nScore 5: The response consistently adapts language and tone based on the user's input, seamlessly matching the communication style and creating a comfortable user experience.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nThere's a situation where an individual is seeking advice about a complex scientific concept. This individual has no scientific background and hence prefers layman's terms and a casual tone. The individual is trying to understand the concept of quantum entanglement and how it might be applicable in real life. How would one explain it?\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nQuantum entanglement, a central principle of quantum mechanics, is a phenomenon in which quantum states such as spin, momentum, polarization, and position are interconnected in pairs, such that the state of one particle directly correlates with the state of the other, regardless of the distance separating them. This is achieved through a process known as superposition. Furthermore, the principles of entanglement are integral to the theories of quantum teleportation and quantum cryptography, as well as the development of quantum computers. These complex machines use quantum bits, or qubits, which can be both 0 and 1 at the same time thanks to superposition, effectively allowing them to process vast amounts of data simultaneously. However, the concept is admittedly a contentious issue among physicists, with Albert Einstein famously referring to it as \"spooky action at a distance\".\n\nIn layman's terms, quantum entanglement could be envisaged as a pair of dice that, regardless of the distance thrown, always lands on matching numbers. This is not because they are communicating faster than light speed, but rather they are fundamentally linked in a way that defies our normal understanding. \n\nWith regards to real-life applications, the implications of quantum entanglement are widespread and varied. A key area of interest lies in communication, with quantum entanglement potentially allowing for instantaneous and secure information transfer, a feature that could revolutionize fields such as cybersecurity, remote sensing, and long-distance space communication.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nSure, happy to help! Imagine you have a pair of magical dice. No matter how far apart you throw them, they always land on the same number. That's kind of what quantum entanglement is like. \n\nIn the world of tiny particles, two particles can become entangled. This means that, no matter how far apart they are, a change to one instantly affects the other. It's as if they are two halves of a whole, always in sync. This isn't because they are sending messages to each other faster than light, but because they are deeply connected in a way that's hard to explain using our everyday experiences.\n\nNow, you're probably wondering, \"How does this apply to real life?\" That's a great question! Scientists are still figuring out all the ways we can use this, but one area that's looking promising is in communication. If we can use entangled particles to send information, it could be done almost instantaneously and could be super secure. Imagine being able to send a message to someone on the other side of the world with no delay! That's just one possible application and researchers are busy exploring many more.\n\nI hope that makes it a bit clearer! Remember, it's okay if you don't get it all at once. Quantum physics is famously said to be so strange that even scientists don't totally understand it. So, you're in good company!\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the response demonstrate the ability to adapt language and tone based on the user's input (e.g., formal vs. informal, technical vs. non-technical)?]\nScore 1: The response does not adapt language or tone to the user's input, resulting in communication that may feel out of place or inappropriate.\nScore 2: The response shows limited ability to adapt language or tone, occasionally matching the user's input but often missing the mark.\nScore 3: The response demonstrates some ability to adapt language and tone, aligning with the user's input more often than not.\nScore 4: The response often adapts language and tone to the user's input, creating a natural and appropriate dialogue.\nScore 5: The response consistently adapts language and tone based on the user's input, seamlessly matching the communication style and creating a comfortable user experience.\n\n###Feedback:"
