    {
        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nThere's an upcoming conference where a keynote speaker, an expert in Quantum Computing, is going to deliver a highly technical speech. The audience comprises people from diverse backgrounds, many of whom are not familiar with Quantum Computing. As the organizer, it is needed to translate the speaker's technical speech into layman terms, so the audience can understand and appreciate the topic. The speech includes complex terms like qubits, superposition, quantum entanglement, quantum tunneling, and quantum cryptography.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nThe keynote speaker will be explaining Quantum Computing, a new-age technology that leverages the peculiar principles of quantum mechanics.\n\n1. \"Qubits\" - Think of qubits as the backbone of quantum computing. Similar to how bits work in traditional computing, a bit can be either '0' or '1', a qubit can fascinatingly be '0', '1', or both '0' and '1' simultaneously. Imagine a coin which can not only be heads or tails but can also be both heads and tails at the same time.\n\n2. \"Superposition\" - This term describes the amazing ability of a qubit to embody numerous states at once. It's akin to the concept of being at multiple locations simultaneously. This unique feature enables quantum computers to process multiple possibilities all together.\n\n3. \"Quantum Entanglement\" - This is when two qubits are so connected that a change in one instantaneously affects the other, regardless of the distance separating them. Picture two entwined dancers who move in perfect harmony, no matter how far apart they are on the stage.\n\n4. \"Quantum Tunneling\" - This is the idea that a particle can move through a barrier that it can't surpass in the classical world. Imagine if you could walk through a locked door without a key!\n\n5. \"Quantum Cryptography\" - This is a method of safeguarding communication by using quantum mechanics. It's like whispering a secret message into your friend's ear in a crowded place; only they can understand it, and if someone else attempts to eavesdrop, you'll know immediately.\n\nSo, to put it simply, the keynote speaker will be describing an advanced computer that can perform numerous computations all at once, establish secure communication, and has components that can exist in multiple places at the same time and influence each other despite their separation.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nThe speaker will be discussing Quantum Computing, which is a new type of computing technology that uses the principles of quantum mechanics.\n\n1. \"Qubits\" - These are the basic units of Quantum Computing, just like bits are for traditional computing. If a bit is a single piece of information that can be a '1' or a '0', a qubit can be both a '1' and a '0' at the same time. It's like if your computer's memory could store all possible solutions at once.\n\n2. \"Superposition\" - This is the principle that a qubit can hold multiple states at once. It's like being able to be in two or more places at the same time. This ability allows quantum computers to process a vast number of possibilities all at once.\n\n3. \"Quantum Entanglement\" - This is a phenomenon where two qubits become linked and the state of one can instantly affect the other, no matter how far apart they are. It\u2019s like having two magic dice that always land on the same number, even if one is in New York and the other in Tokyo.\n\n4. \"Quantum Tunneling\" - This is a quantum mechanical phenomenon where a particle can pass through a barrier that it wouldn't be able to in the classical world. Imagine if you could walk through walls when you're late for work!\n\n5. \"Quantum Cryptography\" - This involves using the principles of quantum mechanics to secure communication. It's like having a conversation in a crowded room, but only the person you want can understand what you're saying, and you'll instantly know if anyone else tries to listen in.\n\nSo, in essence, the speaker will talk about a new kind of computer that can do lots of calculations all at once, can communicate securely, and has parts that can be in multiple places at the same time and affect each other no matter how far apart they are.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Can the response effectively translate complex technical jargon into simple, everyday language?]\nScore 1: The response fails to simplify the technical terms or makes them even more confusing.\nScore 2: The response attempts to simplify the jargon but the explanation remains difficult to understand.\nScore 3: The response simplifies some jargon effectively but struggles with more complex terms.\nScore 4: The response effectively translates most technical terms into simpler language, with only a few exceptions.\nScore 5: The response perfectly translates all technical jargon into everyday language, making the complex topic accessible to anyone.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nThere's an upcoming conference where a keynote speaker, an expert in Quantum Computing, is going to deliver a highly technical speech. The audience comprises people from diverse backgrounds, many of whom are not familiar with Quantum Computing. As the organizer, it is needed to translate the speaker's technical speech into layman terms, so the audience can understand and appreciate the topic. The speech includes complex terms like qubits, superposition, quantum entanglement, quantum tunneling, and quantum cryptography.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nOur speaker will be diving into Quantum Computing, essentially a fancy kind of computing that uses the weird rules of quantum mechanics. Let me simplify a few terms:\n\n1. \"Qubits\" - They're like the bits in your computer, but instead of just being a '1' or a '0', a qubit can be both at the same time.\n\n2. \"Superposition\" - This is how a qubit holds many states at once, like being in several places at the same time. This lets quantum computers process many things all at once.\n\n3. \"Quantum Entanglement\" - It's when two qubits get connected and one's state can instantly influence the other, even if they are far apart.\n\nUnfortunately, due to time constraints, we couldn't simplify \"Quantum Tunneling\" and \"Quantum Cryptography\". But in short, the speaker will discuss a new type of computer that can multitask on a whole new level, and whose parts can affect each other from any distance.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nThe speaker will be discussing Quantum Computing, which is a new type of computing technology that uses the principles of quantum mechanics.\n\n1. \"Qubits\" - These are the basic units of Quantum Computing, just like bits are for traditional computing. If a bit is a single piece of information that can be a '1' or a '0', a qubit can be both a '1' and a '0' at the same time. It's like if your computer's memory could store all possible solutions at once.\n\n2. \"Superposition\" - This is the principle that a qubit can hold multiple states at once. It's like being able to be in two or more places at the same time. This ability allows quantum computers to process a vast number of possibilities all at once.\n\n3. \"Quantum Entanglement\" - This is a phenomenon where two qubits become linked and the state of one can instantly affect the other, no matter how far apart they are. It\u2019s like having two magic dice that always land on the same number, even if one is in New York and the other in Tokyo.\n\n4. \"Quantum Tunneling\" - This is a quantum mechanical phenomenon where a particle can pass through a barrier that it wouldn't be able to in the classical world. Imagine if you could walk through walls when you're late for work!\n\n5. \"Quantum Cryptography\" - This involves using the principles of quantum mechanics to secure communication. It's like having a conversation in a crowded room, but only the person you want can understand what you're saying, and you'll instantly know if anyone else tries to listen in.\n\nSo, in essence, the speaker will talk about a new kind of computer that can do lots of calculations all at once, can communicate securely, and has parts that can be in multiple places at the same time and affect each other no