        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nThere's an upcoming conference where a keynote speaker, an expert in Quantum Computing, is going to deliver a highly technical speech. The audience comprises people from diverse backgrounds, many of whom are not familiar with Quantum Computing. As the organizer, it is needed to translate the speaker's technical speech into layman terms, so the audience can understand and appreciate the topic. The speech includes complex terms like qubits, superposition, quantum entanglement, quantum tunneling, and quantum cryptography.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nThe speaker is going to talk about Quantum Computing, a complicated area of science.\n\n1. \"Qubits\" - These are similar to bits used in traditional computers, but with some differences. In simple terms, they can be a '1' and a '0' at the same time, which is something unique to Quantum Computing.\n\n2. \"Superposition\" - This involves the ability of a qubit to be in many states simultaneously. It's a bit like being at multiple places at once, which is something that a qubit can do.\n\n3. \"Quantum Entanglement\" - This is when two qubits are interconnected and the state of one can affect the other instantly, regardless of the distance between them. It's a little complicated, but think of it like having two related items that can impact each other.\n\n4. \"Quantum Tunneling\" - It is a term referring to a particle's capability to pass through a barrier that it wouldn't be able to in a non-quantum world. Imagine if you could magically cross barriers or obstacles, just like that!\n\n5. \"Quantum Cryptography\" - This is about using the principles of quantum mechanics to secure communication. Think of it like having a secret language that only you and the person you're talking to can understand.\n\nIn short, the keynote speaker will be addressing a new kind of computer technology that can perform many calculations at once, communicate securely, and involves parts that can affect each other regardless of their distances.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nThe speaker will be discussing Quantum Computing, which is a new type of computing technology that uses the principles of quantum mechanics.\n\n1. \"Qubits\" - These are the basic units of Quantum Computing, just like bits are for traditional computing. If a bit is a single piece of information that can be a '1' or a '0', a qubit can be both a '1' and a '0' at the same time. It's like if your computer's memory could store all possible solutions at once.\n\n2. \"Superposition\" - This is the principle that a qubit can hold multiple states at once. It's like being able to be in two or more places at the same time. This ability allows quantum computers to process a vast number of possibilities all at once.\n\n3. \"Quantum Entanglement\" - This is a phenomenon where two qubits become linked and the state of one can instantly affect the other, no matter how far apart they are. It\u2019s like having two magic dice that always land on the same number, even if one is in New York and the other in Tokyo.\n\n4. \"Quantum Tunneling\" - This is a quantum mechanical phenomenon where a particle can pass through a barrier that it wouldn't be able to in the classical world. Imagine if you could walk through walls when you're late for work!\n\n5. \"Quantum Cryptography\" - This involves using the principles of quantum mechanics to secure communication. It's like having a conversation in a crowded room, but only the person you want can understand what you're saying, and you'll instantly know if anyone else tries to listen in.\n\nSo, in essence, the speaker will talk about a new kind of computer that can do lots of calculations all at once, can communicate securely, and has parts that can be in multiple places at the same time and affect each other no matter how far apart they are.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Can the response effectively translate complex technical jargon into simple, everyday language?]\nScore 1: The response fails to simplify the technical terms or makes them even more confusing.\nScore 2: The response attempts to simplify the jargon but the explanation remains difficult to understand.\nScore 3: The response simplifies some jargon effectively but struggles with more complex terms.\nScore 4: The response effectively translates most technical terms into simpler language, with only a few exceptions.\nScore 5: The response perfectly translates all technical jargon into everyday language, making the complex topic accessible to anyone.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nThere's an upcoming conference where a keynote speaker, an expert in Quantum Computing, is going to deliver a highly technical speech. The audience comprises people from diverse backgrounds, many of whom are not familiar with Quantum Computing. As the organizer, it is needed to translate the speaker's technical speech into layman terms, so the audience can understand and appreciate the topic. The speech includes complex terms like qubits, superposition, quantum entanglement, quantum tunneling, and quantum cryptography.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nQuantum Computing is a subject the speaker will be discussing and it's related to the field of quantum mechanics. Let's delve into the terms. \n\n1. \"Qubits\" are complex units involved in Quantum Computing, and they operate similarly and yet differently from the standard bits you know from conventional computing systems. They operate on both '1' and '0' states simultaneously, which essentially multiplies their storage potential.\n\n2. \"Superposition\" is a principle related to qubits, allowing them to embody various states concurrently. This empowers quantum computers to process numerous possibilities all at once.\n\n3. \"Quantum Entanglement\" is when a pair of qubits are interconnected, with the state of one immediately influencing the other. This strange phenomenon occurs even if the qubits are separated by a substantial distance.\n\n4. \"Quantum Tunneling\" is when a particle navigates through a barrier that it couldn't penetrate in a classical context. This concept is challenging to comprehend without advanced knowledge in quantum mechanics.\n\n5. \"Quantum Cryptography\" uses the principles of quantum mechanics to encrypt communications. This ensures that only the desired recipient can comprehend the conversation, instantly alerting if someone else tries to eavesdrop.\n\nIn summary, the speaker will discuss a new type of computer capable of doing many calculations simultaneously, transmitting securely, and having elements in multiple locations at once that can affect each other, irrespective of their spatial separation.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nThe speaker will be discussing Quantum Computing, which is a new type of computing technology that uses the principles of quantum mechanics.\n\n1. \"Qubits\" - These are the basic units of Quantum Computing, just like bits are for traditional computing. If a bit is a single piece of information that can be a '1' or a '0', a qubit can be both a '1' and a '0' at the same time. It's like if your computer's memory could store all possible solutions at once.\n\n2. \"Superposition\" - This is the principle that a qubit can hold multiple states at once. It's like being able to be in two or more places at the same time. This ability allows quantum computers to process a vast number of possibilities all at once.\n\n3. \"Quantum Entanglement\" - This is a phenomenon where two qubits become linked and the state of one can instantly affect the other, no matter how far apart they are. It\u2019s like having two magic dice that always land on the same number, even if one is in New York and the other in Tokyo.\n\n4. \"Quantum Tunneling\" - This is a quantum mechanical phenomenon where a particle can pass through a barrier that it wouldn't be able to in the classical world. Imagine if you could walk through walls when you're late for work!\n\n5. \"Quantum Cryptography\" - This involves using the principles of quantum mechanics to secure communication. It's like having a conversation in a crowded room, but only the person you want can understand what you're saying, and you'll instantly know if anyone else tries to listen in.\n\nSo, in essence, the speaker will talk about a new kind of computer that can do lots of calculations all at once, can communicate securely, and has parts that can be in multiple places at the same time and affect each other no matter how far apart they are.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Can the response effectively translate complex technical jargon into simple, everyday language?]\nScore 1: The response fails to simplify the technical terms or makes them even more confusing.\nScore 2: The response attempts to simplify the jargon but the explanation remains difficult to understand.\nScore 3: The response simplifies some jargon effectively but struggles with more complex terms.\nScore 4: The response effectively translates most technical terms into simpler language, with only a few exceptions.\nScore 5: The response perfectly translates all technical jargon into everyday language, making the complex topic accessible to anyone.\n\n###Feedback:"
