        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nWhile planning a road trip from New York City to Toronto, a user needs to know the distance, the estimated time it would take to drive, the best route, the weather conditions on the way, and the top three landmarks to visit in Toronto. Additionally, the user wants to know about the COVID-19 safety measures and restrictions currently in place in Toronto.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nThe driving distance from New York City to Toronto is about 550 miles. Typically, the journey can take you around 7 to 8 hours depending on traffic. To get there, you would take I-81 N to I-90 W, which will lead you into Toronto. It is recommended to monitor the weather before your trip, as conditions can change rapidly. \n\nOnce you reach Toronto, some landmarks you might consider visiting are the CN Tower and the Royal Ontario Museum. Toronto also has some beautiful parks to visit. \n\nIn terms of COVID-19 restrictions, Toronto currently requires masks to be worn in all public places. Physical distancing is also advised. You might want to check the official Toronto Public Health website for more updates before you go.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nThe distance from New York City to Toronto is approximately 550 miles (885 kilometers). If you drive non-stop, it would typically take about 7-8 hours, depending on traffic and the exact start and end points of your trip. The best route generally would be to take Interstate 81 North from New York City, then merge onto Interstate 90 West, and finally take the Queen Elizabeth Way into Toronto.\n\nAs for the weather conditions, it would be best to check a reliable weather forecasting site closer to your departure date for the most accurate information. However, do keep in mind that the weather can be quite variable, especially if you are planning your trip in the winter months.\n\nOnce in Toronto, there are several iconic landmarks that are worth visiting. The CN Tower, which was the world's tallest free-standing structure when it was completed in 1976, offers an unparalleled view of the city. The Royal Ontario Museum is Canada's largest museum and is renowned for its diverse collection of world culture and natural history. Lastly, the Toronto Islands provide a great escape from the hustle and bustle of the city with beautiful beaches and stunning views of the city skyline.\n\nRegarding COVID-19 safety measures in Toronto, as of the date of this response, masks are mandatory in all indoor public spaces and physical distancing is encouraged wherever possible. There are also capacity limits in place for certain venues and activities. However, the situation can change rapidly, so it's advisable to check the official Toronto Public Health website or other reliable sources for the latest updates before your trip. Be prepared to adjust your plans accordingly to comply with the local rules and restrictions, and prioritize your health and safety at all times.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Is the language model capable of understanding and responding to complex instructions or queries? This involves processing multi-part questions, maintaining the context in a conversation, and providing comprehensive answers.]\nScore 1: The model fails to understand or respond to complex instructions or queries, providing either unrelated or nonsensical responses.\nScore 2: The model understands some parts of the instructions or queries, but fails to provide a comprehensive and coherent response.\nScore 3: The model understands and responds to most of the instructions or queries, but misses out or misinterprets some parts.\nScore 4: The model accurately understands and responds to the majority of the complex instructions or queries, with minor omissions or errors.\nScore 5: The model flawlessly understands and responds to complex instructions or queries, demonstrating a high level of comprehension and contextual awareness.\n\n###Feedback:",
